  "text": "To find the value of the determinant\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), we will use properties of determinants and trigonometric identities.\n\nFirst, let's denote the determinant by \\(D\\):\n\n\\[\nD = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe can use the property of determinants that allows us to factor out common terms from rows or columns. Notice that each element in the second column is a product of \\(\\cos\\) and \\(\\sin\\) terms. We can factor out \\(\\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha\\) from the first row, \\(\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta\\) from the second row, and \\(\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma\\) from the third row:\n\n\\[\nD = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos \\beta \\cos \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin \\beta \\sin \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos \\gamma \\cos \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin \\gamma \\sin \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nD = \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we can use the identity \\(\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\\) to simplify the determinant further. Notice that if we add the first row to the second row and the second row to the third row, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta + \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\\) for \\(\\theta = \\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n1 & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n1 & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta + \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we can factor out \\(\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma\\) from the last row:\n\n\\[\nD = (\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma) \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n1 & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha + \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n1 & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta + \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the determinant in the last row is the same as the determinant in the first row, but with the second and third columns swapped. This means that the determinant is zero because the rows (or columns) are linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the value of the determinant is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]",
